                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "222019a7-86a0-449b-beee-3426e54b1f98",
                        "source_entry_id": "40693159-27c9-376e-8c7e-6e4f618bb15d",
                        "source_sense_id": "8e86ac8a-fce2-3c54-9c7f-dfc08dd1b057",
                        "source_sense_description": "Hexerei nutzender Heiler",
                        "target_lang_word": "witch doctor",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "witch doctor",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                        "entry_id": "86910651-8ee7-3260-b6de-2db6f583af7e",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person (often male) who is believed to ward off witchcraft and heal through magical powers; a shaman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e0a0d8da-2ac1-453b-acff-c5f214395ceb",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "[T]he ſaid Dorothy Durent, having been with a Witch-Doctor, acknowledges upon Oath, that by his Advice, ſhe hang'd up her Child's Blanket in the Chimney, found a Toad in it at Night, had put it into the Fire, and held it there tho' it made a great and horrible Noiſe, and flaſht like Gunpowder, and went off like a Piſtol, and then became inviſible, and that by this the Priſoner was ſcorch'd and burn'd lamentably."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "51a8c3b5-bf6d-4ff3-a4de-ed41ed9b2976",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "Some people are so foolish as to go to a witch-doctor when they have Cows in this complaint [prematurely parting with their unborn calves]:—they give him some money, and he tells them some frivolous tale, such as that some person that wishes them bad luck, or looks upon them with an evil eye, [...]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "711559ef-0f7c-499d-98da-29e4c568b347",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "At that consultation, according to the habit of a witch-doctor, he divined and announced the occasion of their attendance, and indicated the complainant, Mdungazwe, as the wizard who had caused Ranise to suffer."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "af98cef5-cf8c-4ff5-92e9-cdf630c0e96e",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "I told the witch doctor I was in love with you / And then the witch doctor he told me what to do"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "87c53c74-d374-4123-b3c9-8ad77b568d4f",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "Review of the literature cited in the bibliography of the present paper left this author with the impression that there is indeed a very widespread institution variously labelled by Western observers with epithets such as medicine-man, shaman, witch-doctor, native healer, or with indigenous terms such as angakok (Eskimo), or mganga (Swahili)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1fa1e524-9921-4b84-9b36-1352f1fd6c0f",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "In much of popular Western literature, the non-Western professional who attends to the relief of distress and alleviation of anxiety in his society is known by many names, none of them exactly respectful. He is a medicine man or a witch doctor, native healer or voodoo sorcerer—the \"quack,\" so to speak, never the \"doctor.\""
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "749b4b89-2cb8-45aa-96a8-b901d5d8309c",
                                                "sense_id": "978add02-450b-320a-90bf-5ca95828f967",
                                                "text": "The man being led into the Cage did not strike me as a witchdoctor. Slim and tall, he was a middle-aged man with a fuzzy Afro which made him look even younger."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
